---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import VerificationBadge from '../components/VerificationBadge.astro';
import StatusBadge from '../components/StatusBadge.astro';
import { pageTitle, canonicalUrl, graphJsonLd, breadcrumbGraphNode, webPageGraphNode } from '../lib/seo';

const canonicalPath = '/how-we-verify/';
const title = pageTitle('How We Verify Study Groups & Links');
const description = "What verification states mean for exam-prep communities, what link checks cover, and what they don't.";
const pageUrl = canonicalUrl(canonicalPath);
const jsonLd = graphJsonLd([
  breadcrumbGraphNode([{ name: 'How We Verify', path: canonicalPath }], pageUrl),
  webPageGraphNode({ name: title, description, canonicalPath }),
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-3xl">How We Verify</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-stone-600">
      We state clearly what each listing's status means and what it does not cover.
      StudyGroupsHub never implies full safety auditing of private group chats.
    </p>

    <div class="prose-static mt-8">
      <h2>What are the limits of a directory listing?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>
          A working or source-confirmed community link does not mean every study resource,
          tutor, exam claim, or file shared inside that community has been independently
          verified.
        </strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        We verify <em>that a community exists and its invite link appears to work</em>.
        We do not verify everything that happens inside it, and you should not assume we have.
        Treat every community as a place to evaluate for yourself.
      </p>

      <h2>Verification states</h2>
      <p>Every listing carries exactly one verification state:</p>

      <h3><VerificationBadge status="unverified" size="md" /> (Unverified)</h3>
      <p>
        A public invite URL was found, with no additional verification performed.
        This is the default state for most discovered listings.
      </p>

      <h3><VerificationBadge status="source-confirmed" size="md" /> (Source confirmed)</h3>
      <p>
        A public source clearly identifies the community and its invite URL, such as an exam
        study blog, a prep-course page, or a public directory that names the group and links
        its invite. Claims made by the third-party source are not independently re-checked.
      </p>

      <h3><VerificationBadge status="owner-confirmed" size="md" /> (Owner confirmed)</h3>
      <p>
        The community's owner or admin completed an ownership verification step, such as
        confirming the listing from the community's own official channel. This confirms the
        listing belongs to that community's operators, not a review of group chat content.
      </p>

      <h3><VerificationBadge status="manually-reviewed" size="md" /> (Manually reviewed)</h3>
      <p>
        An editor manually reviewed the listing against its public sources: the invite link,
        exam claims, platform, and description. This means a human checked the public
        metadata; it is not a safety certification and does not guarantee shared files are
        authorized.
      </p>

      <h2>What we verify about exam claims</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Exam and certification focus:</strong> A listing is tagged for an exam
          (for example <a href="/exam/usmle/">USMLE</a>, <a href="/exam/cfa/">CFA</a>, or{' '}
          <a href="/exam/pmp/">PMP</a>) only when the community's public materials support that
          claim. We never guess a community's exam focus from its name alone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Platform and invite:</strong> The listed platform (<a href="/platform/discord/">Discord</a>,{' '}
          <a href="/platform/telegram/">Telegram</a>, or WhatsApp) and invite URL must match what
          the public source shows.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Link status:</strong> Whether the invite URL responds as reachable.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Link status</h2>
      <p>Link checks answer one question: does the invite URL appear reachable?</p>
      <ul>
        <li><StatusBadge status="active" />: the URL responded as reachable at the last check.</li>
        <li><StatusBadge status="unknown" />: the URL could not be confidently verified due to blocks, timeouts, or ambiguous responses.</li>
        <li><StatusBadge status="dead" />: repeated strong evidence (such as persistent 404s) shows the invite no longer works.</li>
        <li><StatusBadge status="reported" />: a user reported a problem and the listing is awaiting manual review.</li>
        <li><StatusBadge status="removed" />: the listing was removed by moderation.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Checks run on a schedule, never during the website build, and only against
        publicly observable signals. We never join groups to test them.
      </p>

      <h2>What we never claim</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We never mark a community "safe", "trusted", or "exam-verified".</li>
        <li>We never certify that study materials, dumps, or tutors inside a group are legitimate.</li>
        <li>We never generate ratings, reviews, or pass-rate statistics.</li>
        <li>Member counts appear only when a real platform source provides them.</li>
        <li>Directory discovery is not verification; finding a public URL is only the first step.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Something wrong? Use the <a href="/report/">report form</a> or{' '}
        <a href="/contact/">contact us</a>. Corrections are reviewed and applied by a
        human editor.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
